<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  ============================================================================
  RM_Filth_Tar.xml            FlowWorks · authored 2026-09-24 (SUMP_WALKWAYS_1)
  ============================================================================
  Tracked tar filth — the substance any TerrainDef using RM_Tar (the FlowWorks
  Heavy-viscosity liquid, RM_LiquidDefRegistry.xml) can leave on a pawn's feet
  via vanilla's own TerrainDef.generatedFilth field (Source/Verse/TerrainDef.cs
  line 292) and Pawn_FilthTracker.TryPickupFilth/TryDropFilth (RimWorld/
  Pawn_FilthTracker.cs — read whole this pass). This is the SAME real,
  already-shipping mechanism vanilla uses for e.g. Sand terrain tracking
  Filth_Sand indoors; no new C# generates or tracks this filth, only the
  FilthDef itself and the <generatedFilth> wiring on RM_TarShallow are new
  (RUT_TarShallow_FilthAcceptance.xml, extended this pass).

  Lives in FlowWorks (not the Sump kit) because the tar LIQUID is FlowWorks'
  own (RM_Tar registry row, SUMP_TAR_HYDROLOGY_1's own framing) — the stain it
  leaves on a boot is the same substance's property, not a Sump-specific
  invention, and any future biome that opens an RM_Tar body inherits this for
  free.

  pathCost 34 — CALIBRATED, not guessed: matches RM_GreentideChurnmud's own
  "brutally slow" mire value (RUT_TarMoat.xml's own header, same repo,
  same reasoning), deliberately a real, meaningful movement penalty. Vanilla
  filth normally carries pathCost 0 (checked: Filth_Dirt/Filth_Sand/every
  BaseFilth-derived def in Data/Core/Defs/ThingDefs_Misc/Filth.xml sets none),
  so filth does not slow movement at all by default — PathGrid.
  CalculatedCostAt (Source/Verse/AI/PathGrid.cs:120) takes the MAX of terrain
  pathCost and every Thing on the cell's own def.pathCost, so a nonzero
  pathCost here is what turns "tar filth present" into "duckboards fouled and
  slow" with zero new C#. This is SUMP_WALKWAYS_1's own duckboards mechanism
  (item spec §1, "fouls with tar... accumulating filth slows it").

  disappearsInDays deliberately OMITTED (defaults FloatRange.Zero → 0 ticks →
  Filth.DisappearAfterTicks stays 0 → SteadyEnvironmentEffects.cs:128's own
  `DisappearAfterTicks != 0` guard never fires): tar filth never fades on its
  own. "Until scrubbed" (his spec) means scrubbed, not weathered — cleaning
  (JobDriver_Clean, FilthMaker.RemoveAllFilth via the normal cleaning job) is
  the only way off. rainWashes also left at its default false for the same
  reason — this is deliberately worse than vanilla mud, matching
  SUMP_TAR_NASTINESS_1's own tuning law ("full cleanliness should be a losing
  fight, not an achievable state").

  cleaningWorkToReduceThickness 60 — INVENTED, above Filth_Dirt/Filth_Sand's
  own 35, reflecting "tar-proof/tar-fouled" being harder to scrub than plain
  dirt. Flag for live tuning.

  placementMask Terrain — required for FilthProperties.TerrainSourced (true
  only when placementMask has the Terrain flag, RimWorld/FilthProperties.cs)
  so Pawn_FilthTracker remembers this as lastTerrainFilthDef and keeps
  tracking it forward across clean cells, exactly like Filth_Dirt/Filth_Sand.

  🔴 Texture path corrected this pass, caught by validate_patch.py, not
  guessed around: a first draft reused vanilla's own Things/Filth/Grainy
  verbatim (the same placeholder reasoning RUT_TarMoat.xml gives for a
  TERRAIN texPath) — but validate_patch.py refuses that as a hard ERROR for
  a ThingDef, exactly the trap RUT_Filth_MouseTrack.xml's own header already
  documents (SUMP_MECHANICS_1 S4, 2026-09-14): once a mod ships ANY content
  under Textures/Things/, the validator treats Things/ as THAT mod's own
  claimed namespace and requires a real file, not a vanilla fallback.
  Repointed to a new RM_-named path under the same vanilla-mirroring folder
  (Things/Filth/RM_FilthTar).

  ART DELIVERED, SUMP_TAR_NASTINESS_1 (2026-09-24), DEPLOY_HOLD LIFTED:
  graphicClass Graphic_Cluster reads texPath as a FOLDER, not a single file
  base name (Verse/Graphic_Collection.cs's own Init: `ContentFinder<
  Texture2D>.GetAllInFolder(req.path)`, confirmed against the decompile) —
  so the real content is three simple placeholder blob sprites at
  Textures/Things/Filth/RM_FilthTar/RM_FilthTar_{a,b,c}.png, procedurally
  drawn irregular dark splotches with soft alpha edges (roughly matching
  this def's own graphicData <color>), not the hand-authored art a real
  review pass would ship. Real (reviewed, hand-composed) art is still owed
  — flagged, not silently treated as done.
  ============================================================================
-->
<Defs>

  <ThingDef ParentName="BaseFilth">
    <defName>RM_Filth_Tar</defName>
    <label>tracked tar</label>
    <description>Black, sticky, and tracked in on someone's boots. It does not wash off, and it does not forgive a cheap floor.</description>
    <altitudeLayer>Filth</altitudeLayer>
    <category>Filth</category>
    <drawerType>MapMeshOnly</drawerType>
    <filth>
      <cleaningSound>Interact_CleanFilth_Dirt</cleaningSound>
      <placementMask>
        <li>Terrain</li>
      </placementMask>
      <cleaningWorkToReduceThickness>60</cleaningWorkToReduceThickness>
      <canFilthAttach>true</canFilthAttach>
    </filth>
    <graphicData>
      <graphicClass>Graphic_Cluster</graphicClass>
      <shaderType>Transparent</shaderType>
      <texPath>Things/Filth/RM_FilthTar</texPath>
      <color>(18,16,14,150)</color>
    </graphicData>
    <statBases>
      <Beauty>-18</Beauty>
      <Cleanliness>-6</Cleanliness>
      <BeautyOutdoors>-2</BeautyOutdoors>
    </statBases>
    <thingClass>Filth</thingClass>
    <useHitPoints>false</useHitPoints>
    <pathCost>34</pathCost>
  </ThingDef>

</Defs>
